ype/radio :answer-field/correct "q-77888/selection/01653be8a31cda10308bcaaee7dd78889374fcd01c103268b9bc0b852aa5f48a" :answer-field/choices ["q-77888/selection/f4288a1e1e7b3cd434deaaa7015cb6d327e588af8ba3d59126ebe640a8019788" "q-77888/selection/1394bf5ea57bbdc81c04559bd9cd52b63d042c245e8785f0b1a4765edc8d3371" "q-77888/selection/35f5a934831a79f042328b3b2ce94d55968e5b190c5d69a5468396a6a9ae1dda" "q-77888/selection/01653be8a31cda10308bcaaee7dd78889374fcd01c103268b9bc0b852aa5f48a" "q-77888/selection/7e8145e5e213ae6912db954e445390ab18658fa02502788921812208ca96de52"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-77888" :question/id #uuid "58937f0c-40a0-5a69-a5bf-d0139202fa4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-77888" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,3]?$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,3],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,3]$ and differentiable on $(-1,3).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $h(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,3]$ and differentiable on $(-1,3),$ so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Hence, $f(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,3].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $p(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,3]$ but has a corner point at $x=2.$ Hence, $p(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=2,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,3).$ Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)$  has a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ Hence, $r(x)$  is not continuous on $[-1,3].$ Therefore, $r(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $t(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,3]$ but has a corner point at $x=0.$ Hence, $t(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,3).$ Therefore, $t(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-77888/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "999d8587-8f15-5871-81dd-26c0c0ae063e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-77888"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-125317/selection/f0e45b470dfd466e6fe7d9e0a1f75494bcd029a057251f9f152e191a4a7de5c4" :answer/id #uuid "babd29ad-332f-5bf1-a097-5410fc085503" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/09017ce160ebb2995e0c7c4efe81fd5444ba8cedd91dc270c69757b8b0eb1ae4.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125317/selection/a9203e0e8f9faa910a9215e816b8a1f8e63f592e9582c9df5a56fefbeb8f567d" :answer/id #uuid "aee0cde4-4274-5c11-a1a6-35951fd3a6b3" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/c4c45b3d88dd9821c1c5650e898d806b8887838c609548296040feae8915e40b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125317/selection/3125d6d27eb3e7ae10143a6272fc4abf66bb55d8eddcc44e366cb77b5a89d0d2" :answer/id #uuid "eb037793-c00e-51a0-b7e9-19ff2a8b5b7a" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/b246b2835d856e4ec43bd9a96275ddf5531b7d653d46e5c58ae9553b6b59956e.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125317/selection/5e4e3897ab9ee122eae7863e3afae219029678894b507989bc783efe130d9f0a" :answer/id #uuid "28f4edb0-b2e0-5f2d-b31b-e20351847375" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/45db5f9f600f29e91fb40335f80fb28bb12f87fc4618c623354b91408f6bb09c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125317/s