nd differentiable on $(0,2).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $t(x)$  has a vertical asymptote at $x=1$ and is not defined for $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $t(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)$  is continuous on $[0,2]$ but has a cusp at $x=1.$  Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(0,2).$  Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$ Hence, it  is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $s(x)$ is continuous on $[0,2]$ but has a corner point at $x=1.$   Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(0,2).$  Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-48399/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "999d8587-8f15-5871-81dd-26c0c0ae063e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-48399"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-77875/selection/141e8263405b4d00976f4e44bbab4fa3ab0d1aa9f6188376a8cdccdcae97af60" :answer/id #uuid "5f1bc938-fde1-5e2f-98d0-fa7c510c44e6" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/bf01326a229d79ae6cf4fce2f4bc39a08c91a49a287f36562e1e86d1e00b0e20.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-77875/selection/0b534f450340f8ec6ea1a8a72efff7d726ff11db662a17647d08ac835775429e" :answer/id #uuid "ae6b5347-fefb-5fb2-b6ca-0adf30349f42" :answer/type :answer.type/im