5ce1c4a" "q-77875/selection/6bb6ef44d874474df6a1395c1687e59fcf350531dcbd84c011b27cdb24b37920"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-77875" :question/id #uuid "0c729845-96ed-5d93-967e-f4a533dc7da3" :question/math-academy-id "q-77875" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,5]?$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,5],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,5]$ and differentiable on $(-1,5).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $p(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,5]$ and differentiable on $(-1,5)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=-1.$ Hence, $f(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,5].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)$  is continuous on $[-1,5]$ but has a vertical tangent at $x=2.$  Hence, $g(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=2,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,5).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $q(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Hence, $q(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,5].$ Therefore, $q(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,5]$ but has corner points at $x=0$ and $x=4.$ Hence, $r(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0$ and $x=4,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,5).$ Therefore, $r(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-77875/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "999d8587-8f15-5871-81dd-26c0c0ae063e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-77875"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-48396/s