0797e8c5c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-48396/selection/beadbc205f1b3e461be3b7e2ab8ecd72af4ed645a2d763af03e7e6f5b8a62d5b" :answer-field/choices ["q-48396/selection/beadbc205f1b3e461be3b7e2ab8ecd72af4ed645a2d763af03e7e6f5b8a62d5b" "q-48396/selection/d2d4d3662c9a79c48ee0463b25af41169ad10185d7a46fbcf1b58dd3d535aeb4" "q-48396/selection/310992f38dc927a6a688922cbc6109e194db5c4254d1a23a962eebe3bbe71729" "q-48396/selection/a2c861c56da5e42f9e0882c048b8dc1a2b18c1769b4469b6345aee5f28f439c4" "q-48396/selection/53db95d9aee864dcda984a00d294b2162fdd804d81044e79afb86206c01501eb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-48396" :question/id #uuid "8670d161-54b2-5d76-9f17-77b18f090a9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-48396" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,4]?$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,4],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $t(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function  $g(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,4]$ but has a corner point at $x=0.$   Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(1,4).$  Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $s(x)$  has a vertical asymptote at $x=1$ and is not defined for $x\\in (-\\infty ,1].$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[-1,4].$ Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=3.$ Hence, $h(x)$  is not continuous on $[-1,4].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $f(x)$  is continuous on $[-1,4]$ but has a cusp at $x=2.$  Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=2,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,4).$  Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-48396/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "999d8587-8f15-5871-81dd-26c0c0ae063e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-48396"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127013/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "be200f29-1998-5d37-bf05-22e22786efd4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127013/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "028e6e8b-8793-5ec9-91d1-c87713cb4e5f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127013/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "99a27541-8b8e-5648-a51c-72fd6996b406" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127013/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "c81cbbd4-4e7c-5e03-93f6-fab148bd5f7f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127013/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "58bb6da6-8fe0-5437-a563-3216c42738e6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127013/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "468bce35-a6af-512c-a602-d116db5280cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127013/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-127013/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-127013/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-127013/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-127013/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-127013/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127013" :question/id #uuid "d78e600b-4326-5596-a935-0576240419b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-127013" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n\n- $f(x)=2|x|$\n- $g(x)=|x-1|$\n- $h(x)=|x+1|$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=2|x|$ is continuous on $[-1,1],$ but it has a corner in $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x-1|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=1-x$ over the given interval. Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=1$ but this point does not lie inside $(-1,1).$\n\n- The function $h(x)=|x+1|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=x+1$ over the given interval. Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=h(x)$ has a corner point at $x=-1$ but this point does not lie inside $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, only functions II and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-127013/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127013"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-48497/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "db8e3319-4087-53ea-ab8e-545a388b1106" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48497/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "99185650-3aca-5788-80ed-0299e529036e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48497/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "ff342493-7ac1-5c81-9e90-11e24667c2dc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48497/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "350d3a49-76f7-5443-8488-eac6e90e4e15" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48497/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "5c246d93-1a7e-5ac0-bc7c-075112e3ff7c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48497/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d7271472-91ee-5dab-9fa6-a3d4527d95f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-48497/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-48497/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-48497/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-48497/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-48497/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-48497/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-48497" :question/id #uuid "ad37b0b8-9076-54d3-a5c8-dee0a591a349" :question/math-academy-id "q-48497" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,4]?$\n\n- $g(x)=|x-1|$\n- $f(x)={x}^{4}$\n- $h(x)=\\sqrt{x-1}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,4],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,4]$ and differentiable on $(0,4).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x-1|$ is continuous on $[0,4]$ but has a corner at $x=1.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(0,4).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{4}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[0,4]$ and differentiable on $(0,4).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\sqrt{x-1}$ is not defined on $(-\\infty ,1).$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,4].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only function II satisfies the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,4].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-48497/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-48497"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-125262/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "40cf4195-fa54-545b-a7a2-3922c6699af0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125262/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "b3c11f10-26e9-5867-87d6-01838c8eebae" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125262/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "780330c2-6dd6-55b6-81a5-ff4de9c12b25" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125262/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "6b6ccab4-a34e-52dd-85e1-d7214cc3bfa5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125262/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "d810bbd6-15e1-5441-a5b8-99651b3eea8b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125262/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "83ca8298-6a28-5a18-8212-0ae536c8648f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-125262/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-125262/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-125262/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-125262/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-125262/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-125262/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-125262" :question/id #uuid "0d26c1fd-9972-5992-b3ee-1cc9fed13d91" :question/math-academy-id "q-125262" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,e]?$\n\n- $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$\n- $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $h(x)={e}^{-x/2}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,e],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,e]$ and differentiable on $(0,e).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[0,e]$ and differentiable on $(0,e).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so it is not continuous at $x=0,$ which means it is not continuous on $[0,e].$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)={e}^{-x/2}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[0,e]$ and differentiable on $(0,e).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,e].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-125262/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-125262"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-48499"] :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n\n- $g(x)=|x|$\n- $s(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$\n- $h(x)=2x$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ but has a corner at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is not defined at $x=0$, so it is not continuous at $x=0$, which means it is not continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only function III satisfies the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-127014/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "d9bdeeec-7878-57c0-a3d3-290aab7782bb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127014/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "dd460f4e-4fe7-5931-8275-17aa12236be4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127014/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "e55c8538-8410-539f-8770-7a2682e86508" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127014/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "cac70e6a-f7ad-50a8-8322-28dc617ec95e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127014/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "ab198bc9-1f18-5552-b9d9-d52ef9123f99" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127014/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a5bd924-cec8-57d3-bd28-99edc3da23a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127014/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-127014/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-127014/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-127014/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-127014/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-127014/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127014" :question/id #uuid "937932cc-d4b5-5d08-ac33-37cd1b49f559" :question/math-academy-id "q-127014" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3]?$\n\n- $p(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $q(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$\n- $r(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x-1|)$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3],$ the function must be continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $p(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(0,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$ Therefore, $p(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $q(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(1,\\infty ).$ Given that it is not defined at $x=1,$ $q(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$ Given that it is not defined at $x=1,$ $r(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only function I satisfies the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-127014/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127014"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3718"] :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ below is continuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4).$  Complete the sentence below.\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c \\in (-1,4)$ such that $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ \\, .$ *\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/5672cc68e55078623f34f01650d3e82bd3cee62e6ca97fabcd2287f2a865501a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b),$ the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n*There exists a point $c \\in (a,b)$ such that\n$f'(c) = \\dfrac {f(b) - f(a)} {b-a}.$*\n\nIn our case, we have $a = -1$ and $b=4,$ so the above statement becomes \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(-1)}{4-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-2}{4+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c \\in (-1,4)$ such that* $f'(c) =\\dfrac {4}{5}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3717"] :question/problem "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for  $f(x) = x^2$ on the interval $[0,2].$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$  is a polynomial, so it is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2).$ \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c \\in (0,2)$ such that \n\n$$\nf'(c)   = \\dfrac{f(b) - f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=0$ and $b=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(2)-f(0)}{2-(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf'(c) = 2c.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2c & =2 \\\\ c & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3719"] :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[−2,2]?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/e680c2e80874a4c2e8b4d31fcc7a6ba42a3c55fb7c6f559e42f3cd785b22e3ed.png)" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{I}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the sharp corners at $x=\\pm 2$ are not in $(-2,2).$\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{II}$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-2.$ So it is not continuous on $[-2,2],$ and therefore it does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{III}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2),$ so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that although the function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2,$ it is right-continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{IV}$ is continuous on $[−2,2]$ but has a vertical tangent at $x=0.$ So, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(−2,2).$  Therefore, it does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nIn conclusion, only diagrams $\\textrm{I}$ and $\\textrm{III}$ show functions that satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2].$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6738"] :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1]?$\n\n- $f(x)= 3^{x} - 1$\n\n- $g(x)= x^3 - x^2 + 1$\n\n- $h(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 3^{x} - 1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)= x^3 - x^2 + 1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is not defined at $x = 0,$ hence, it is not continuous at \n$x = 0,$ and so it is not continuous on $[0,1].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only the functions I and II satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1].$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
